title: CPK — the hub, the spokes and who owns them

@cpk_audit_2024
# prose-audit: Grade A — Lasek, Horała and the displaced villages carry the audit as a political fight over what the money is for; rail-first, conditional rescue, full disclosure and both opposition routes end in authored consequences rather than a verdict on the project.
title: The Central Communications Port goes under audit
subtitle: Rail for towns that lost their trains, an airport for a prestige fight, and a land programme nobody wants to defend
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 25
order: 12
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/cpk-baranow-2017.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and cpk_stage = 0 and ((year = 2024 and month >= 3) or year >= 2025)
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_stage = 1;
Q.cpk_audit_open = 1;
Q.news_headline = Q.government_party == "pis"
    ? "The government defends CPK against its own cost estimates"
    : "The new plenipotentiary opens the books on CPK";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — The largest investment in the country, and nobody agrees what it is

The **Central Communications Port** is three projects wearing one name. A hub
airport at Baranów, forty kilometres west of Warsaw. Roughly two thousand
kilometres of new railway — the "spokes" — including a tunnel under Łódź and a
high-speed line towards Poznań and Wrocław. And a land programme that has been
buying out villages for years under a special act written to make refusal
expensive.

[? if government_party = "pis": The cabinet still belongs to Prawo i
Sprawiedliwość, and **Marcin Horała**, the deputy minister who has run the
project since 2020, treats every published cost estimate as an act of sabotage.
The company's own traffic forecasts have moved; the delivery date has not.?][?
if government_party != "pis": **Maciej Lasek** — Civic Platform deputy, aviation
engineer, the man who led the state investigation into the Smoleńsk crash — is
the new government's plenipotentiary for the project. His audit covers land
prices, contracts, traffic forecasts and the schedule inherited from **Marcin
Horała**. PiS has already decided what the audit means: a foreign-serving
government killing a Polish investment.?]

Around our table nobody defends the airport with much passion. The railway is
different. Half the towns our organisers work in lost their passenger service
between 1990 and 2005 and have been waiting for a timetable ever since. The
land programme is different again: farmers in Baranów and Wiskitki have spent
five years being told what their houses are worth by a state company.

[? if left_in_government = 1: We are inside the cabinet that owns the answer,
which means the audit will be read as ours whatever it concludes.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We are outside the decision, which frees us to define
what a serious answer would look like before the government publishes one.?]

- @cpk_audit_rail_first
- @cpk_audit_conditions
- @cpk_audit_transparency
- @cpk_audit_scrutiny
- @cpk_audit_defend

@cpk_audit_rail_first
title: Make the audit answer one question — which lines, and when
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Force a published rail timetable ahead of the airport decision, and accept that the airport lobby will call it an obituary.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot staff a parallel transport team inside the review.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.cpk_rail_priority += 20;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 4;
Q.cpk_delivery += 2;
Q.cpk_left_position = "Railway first, airport judged on its own numbers";
Q.rural_support += 2;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Government proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers plenipotentiary and the transport ministry";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Rail component prioritised inside the review";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica turns the CPK audit into a railway timetable";
!}

Our people go into the review with one demand written on the first page: a
dated list of lines, with the towns named. Not corridors, not phases — station
names and years. The airport can be argued about afterwards by people who enjoy
arguing about airports.

The effect is immediate and slightly ridiculous. Journalists who have covered
the project for six years discover they cannot say when a train will reach
Sieradz. The aviation press treats the demand as a funeral notice for the hub.
Our own transport people are quietly delighted: for the first time the argument
is about whether the state can run a railway programme rather than about
whether Poland deserves a large airport.

- @poland_event_queue: File the rail question

@cpk_audit_conditions
title: Rescue the whole programme, but attach our conditions to it
subtitle: Keep hub and rail alive in exchange for state ownership and a collective agreement covering every site.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_public_ownership += 12;
Q.cpk_labour_standard += 18;
Q.cpk_delivery += 3;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 3;
Q.cpk_left_position = "Build it, own it publicly, and bargain collectively on site";
Q.labor_credibility += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.psl_relation += 1;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Negotiated concession";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Coalition agreement inside the Council of Ministers";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Ownership and labour conditions written into the programme decision";
Q.news_headline = "The Left saves CPK and writes its own conditions into it";
!}

We do not ask for the project to be smaller. We ask for it to be ours: the
company stays in state hands, the contracts carry a sectoral agreement, and the
State Labour Inspection gets an office on the site rather than a telephone
number. In exchange, Lewica stops briefing against the airport.

The Prime Minister's staff accept within a week, because a coalition partner who
offers to defend the most attacked investment in the country is worth more than
one who complains about it. Razem's transport group calls it buying a PiS
monument with a union stamp on the side, and they are not entirely wrong. What
we have bought is the principle that the biggest construction site in Europe
will not be built by subcontractors nobody can name.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the conditions

@cpk_audit_transparency
title: Demand the land files in full — prices, valuations, buyers
subtitle: Publish what the villages were paid and who profited around the site, whoever that embarrasses.
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_land_conflict += 8;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 5;
Q.cpk_transparency = 1;
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.local_network += 2;
Q.rural_support += 2;
Q.pis_relation -= 4;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.ko_relation -= 1;
    Q.government_coalition_dissent += 1;
}
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Parliamentary and public disclosure demand";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Land register and valuation files opened to inspection";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica demands every CPK land price on one public register";
!}

Every plot, every valuation, every date, every purchaser. Our lawyers write the
request so that it cannot be answered with a summary, and our press people
publish the first tranche themselves rather than waiting for a ministry to
schedule a briefing.

The register is duller than the rumours and worse than the denials. There is no
single scandal in it; there are hundreds of farmers who took a price they were
told was final, a scattering of well-informed purchases nearby, and a state
company that recorded the difference without comment. PiS accuses us of
smearing a national investment. In Baranów, three residents' committees that
have never spoken to a left-wing party ask for a meeting.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the register

@cpk_audit_scrutiny
title: Put the plenipotentiary in front of a committee
subtitle: Use the Sejm's scrutiny power on contracts and forecasts we cannot yet rewrite.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_left_credit += 3;
Q.cpk_audit_scrutiny_done = 1;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.institutional_issue_salience += 2;
Q.pis_relation -= 3;
Q.labor_credibility += 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Sejm infrastructure committee scrutiny";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Committee questions turn the CPK forecasts into a public argument";
!}

We cannot decide the project from opposition, so we do the thing an opposition
can actually do: schedule the hearings, ask for the traffic model, ask which
version of the model was used for the last press conference, and ask why the
two differ.

The plenipotentiary answers for four hours. Nothing collapses; forecasts rarely
collapse in a committee room. But the sitting produces a transcript that every
later cost revision will be measured against, and our transport spokesperson
stops being a person with an opinion about railways and becomes the person who
asked the question first.

- @poland_event_queue: Schedule the hearing

@cpk_audit_defend
title: Defend the railway from outside the government
subtitle: Refuse to let an audit of the airport quietly bury the lines to towns that lost their trains.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_rail_priority += 12;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 4;
Q.cpk_left_position = "Railway defended from opposition, airport left to its authors";
Q.rural_support += 3;
Q.workers_support += 1;
Q.local_network += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition parliamentary and public campaign";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica campaigns for the spokes while the hub is audited";
!}

Our MPs go to the towns on the map rather than to the site of the airport:
Sieradz, Ostrołęka, the stations on the Łódź line that close for the third time
in a generation. The message is not complicated. Whatever happens to the hub,
the country still owes these places a train.

It is a strange position to hold from opposition — defending part of a
government programme against the government auditing it — and both larger
parties find it inconvenient. Local organisers report the first useful
consequence: mayors who have spent four years dealing exclusively with PiS
transport officials now telephone us instead.

- @poland_event_queue: Take the campaign to the line

@cpk_programme_decision_2024
# prose-audit: Grade A — The programme decision keeps hub, staged hub, rail-only and outright cancellation as live routes with their own costs; a Left prime minister is required to cancel, and the opposition routes shape the decision without pretending to make it.
title: The government settles what CPK actually is
subtitle: Hub and spokes, spokes alone, or a cancellation that will be argued about until 2035
new-page: true
