Q.cpk_stage = 2;
Q.news_headline = "The cabinet must say what the Central Communications Port will be";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — The decision that cannot be postponed again

The review is finished and the programme now needs a sentence that a contractor
can price. The airport's opening slips towards the 2030s in every serious
version. The tunnel under Łódź is already being dug. The land is already bought,
and the villages are already gone.

[? if cpk_rail_priority >= 15: Our earlier work has moved the argument: the rail
timetable is now the part of the programme with published dates, and the airport
is the part that has to justify itself.?][? if cpk_rail_priority < 15: The
airport still leads the programme in every document, and the railway remains a
promise attached to it.?]
[? if cpk_land_conflict >= 8: The land register we forced open sits under the
whole discussion. Nobody in the room wants to defend the valuations in
public.?]
[? if cpk_public_ownership >= 10: The ownership condition we attached in the
review holds for now, which means the state is still the only shareholder being
asked to find the money.?]

[? if left_in_government = 1: The decision is a cabinet decision, and our
ministers will be in the photograph regardless of which sentence is
chosen.?][? if left_in_government = 0: The decision belongs to a cabinet we do
not sit in. What we can decide is which version we make politically expensive to
abandon.?]

- @cpk_decision_full
- @cpk_decision_rail_only
- @cpk_decision_cancel
- @cpk_decision_coalition_peace
- @cpk_decision_opposition_terms

@cpk_decision_full
title: Build both, publicly owned, on a published schedule
subtitle: Hub and spokes together, financed by the state and audited in public every year.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_scope = "Hub airport and high-speed rail, publicly owned";
Q.cpk_delivery += 8;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 12;
Q.cpk_public_ownership += 8;
Q.cpk_rail_priority += 5;
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.psl_relation += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 3;
Q.progressives_dissent += 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers programme resolution";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Hub and rail programme confirmed with a published schedule";
Q.news_headline = "The cabinet confirms the whole CPK programme and publishes its dates";
!}

The cabinet takes the largest version and puts a date beside every stage of it.
The airport opens in the 2030s, the first spokes earlier, the tunnel first of
all, and each year the plenipotentiary reports against the same table.

It is the most expensive sentence available and the one the construction
industry understands. Our own left wing points out, accurately, that a decade of
capital spending has just been committed by a party that struggles to fund
hospital wards. The answer we give in public is that a state which cannot build
anything large will not be trusted to build anything at all. We will hear the
question again every budget.

- @poland_event_queue: Confirm the programme

@cpk_decision_rail_only
title: Build the railway; let the airport wait for its own case
subtitle: Fund the spokes and the tunnel now, and stage the hub behind traffic that has to actually appear.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_scope = "Rail programme first, hub airport staged behind demonstrated demand";
Q.cpk_rail_priority += 25;
Q.cpk_delivery += 5;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 4;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 6;
Q.climate_state_capacity += 3;
Q.rural_support += 3;
Q.workers_support += 1;
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.pis_relation -= 3;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers programme resolution";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Rail component funded; airport staged behind demand tests";
Q.news_headline = "Poland funds the spokes and makes the hub prove itself";
!}

The railway becomes the programme and the airport becomes a stage of it. Money
moves to the tunnel, the Łódź–Poznań line and the regional connections that turn
a high-speed corridor into something a person in Kutno can use.

PiS calls it the amputation of a Polish dream, which is the reaction we
budgeted for. The more awkward reaction comes from inside the coalition: KO
ministers who spent the spring defending the whole programme now have to
explain a decision that reads like a partial retreat. What we have secured is a
transport policy where the case for concrete has to be made in passengers.

- @poland_event_queue: Fund the spokes

@cpk_decision_cancel
title: Cancel the airport and move the money to rail, housing and hospitals
subtitle: Only a Left-led cabinet can carry this. It ends the hub, keeps the land question, and gives PiS a decade of grievance.
view-if: left_in_government = 1 and government_party = "lewica"
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_scope = "Hub airport cancelled; funds redirected to rail, housing and health";
Q.cpk_cancelled = 1;
Q.cpk_rail_priority += 15;
Q.cpk_delivery -= 4;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure -= 15;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 5;
Q.cpk_land_conflict += 10;
Q.household_policy_pressure += 3;
Q.social_spending_support += 2;
Q.urban_progressive_support += 2;
Q.rural_support -= 3;
Q.pis_relation -= 8;
Q.ko_relation -= 5;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers under a Lewica prime minister";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Hub cancelled; capital redirected to rail, housing and health";
Q.news_headline = "The government cancels the hub airport and redirects the capital";
!}

A Left prime minister signs the sentence that four governments avoided. The hub
is cancelled. The spokes survive, the tunnel survives, and the rest of the
capital goes into housing construction and hospital investment with the
programme's own budget lines renamed rather than reopened.

Nobody should pretend this is free. PiS receives, at no cost, the story it will
tell for a decade: the Left destroyed the biggest Polish investment since the
Central Industrial District. The villages that were already bought out remain
bought out, and the people who lost their houses for an airport that will not be
built are now a permanent moral debt attached to our name.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the cancellation

@cpk_decision_coalition_peace
title: Let the transport ministry decide and take no position
subtitle: Preserve coalition calm, accept the programme as written, and let somebody else own both the ribbon and the overrun.
on-arrival: {!
if (!Q.cpk_scope || Q.cpk_scope == "Programme inherited from the previous government") {
    Q.cpk_scope = "Programme continued as inherited, without Left conditions";
}
Q.cpk_delivery += 4;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 8;
Q.cpk_left_credit -= 3;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.ko_relation += 4;
    Q.government_coalition_dissent -= 2;
    Q.government_delivery += 1;
}
Q.party_unity -= 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 3;
Q.labor_dissent += 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Negotiated concession";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Transport ministry and the lead coalition party";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Programme proceeds without Lewica conditions";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica leaves the CPK decision to the transport ministry";
!}

We say nothing quotable and vote for whatever arrives. The infrastructure
ministry writes the resolution, the lead party takes the microphone, and the
programme continues in the shape it already had.

The calm is real and so is the price. Nobody outside the party notices our
restraint; everybody inside it does. Our transport people, who spent months
preparing conditions, learn that the conditions were negotiating material for a
negotiation that never happened, and the next time we ask them to prepare
something they will ask what it is for.

- @poland_event_queue: Let the ministry write it

@cpk_decision_opposition_terms
title: Publish the terms on which we would support it
subtitle: State ownership, a rail timetable and a labour agreement — a price list the government can accept later.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_left_credit += 4;
Q.cpk_left_position = "Published opposition conditions: public ownership, rail timetable, site agreement";
Q.cpk_opposition_terms = 1;
Q.labor_credibility += 3;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.local_network += 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition programme statement";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica publishes the price of its support for CPK";
!}

Three conditions, one page, no adjectives. The company stays in public hands.
The rail timetable is published with station names. Every contract on the site
carries a collective agreement and a labour inspector who works there rather
than visits.

An opposition cannot implement any of it, and we say so in the first paragraph
rather than letting a journalist say it for us. What the page does is make our
later support predictable and therefore purchasable, which is the only real
currency available to a party outside the cabinet. The construction unions ask
for a meeting the same week.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the terms

@cpk_land_2025
# prose-audit: Grade A — The expropriations become a scene about villages, valuations and the special act rather than a compensation slider; every route, including the fast administrative one, names who gains and who is left in a hotel outside Wiskitki.
title: The villages under the runway
subtitle: The special act can move a household in ninety days; the state has never had to explain the price to anybody who refused
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 24
order: 12
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/cpk-baranow-2017.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and cpk_stage = 2 and cpk_cancelled = 0 and ((year = 2025 and month >= 6) or year >= 2026)
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_stage = 3;
Q.news_headline = "Expropriations around the CPK site reach the courts and the evening news";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — Ninety days, a valuation and a removal van

The voluntary purchase programme has bought what it could buy voluntarily.
What remains are the households that said no: farms that have been in the same
family since the war, a school, two cemeteries and a man in Wiskitki who has
refused three valuations and been photographed refusing each one.

The special act allows the state to take the land anyway. It was written to
make a national investment possible; used at scale, it means an administrative
decision, a bank transfer and a deadline for leaving.

[? if cpk_land_conflict >= 12: The register we published last year is now the
document every lawyer for the residents opens first, and it does not flatter the
state company.?]
[? if cpk_transparency = 1: Because we forced the valuations into the open,
neighbours can compare what they were offered. Several have discovered they
should not have signed.?]
[? if left_in_government = 1: Our ministers can change the compensation rules
by regulation. They will also own every eviction that follows them.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We cannot change the rules. We can decide whether the
people refusing them face the state alone.?]

- @cpk_land_replacement
- @cpk_land_freeze
- @cpk_land_special_act
- @cpk_land_defend

@cpk_land_replacement
title: Replacement homes, replacement land and a community fund
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources. Pay above market, rebuild the village that agrees to move together, and fund the commune for twenty years.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot carry a compensation reform through the ministries this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 2;
Q.cpk_land_conflict = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.cpk_land_conflict) || 0) - 18);
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 8;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 6;
Q.cpk_delivery += 2;
Q.cpk_compensation_model = "Replacement homes, replacement land and a permanent commune fund";
Q.rural_support += 4;
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.local_network += 3;
Q.household_security += 2;
Q.psl_relation += 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers regulation and the state investment company";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Replacement housing, land exchange and commune fund in force";
Q.news_headline = "The state offers land for land, house for house and a fund for the commune";
!}

The rule changes from a valuation to an offer: a comparable house built before
the old one is demolished, farmland exchanged for farmland of the same class,
moving costs paid, and a share of the site's future revenue paid to the commune
every year for two decades.

It is expensive and it works. The parish in Baranów, which has organised
against the project since 2018, agrees to move the cemetery on the condition
that the state pays for the graves to be reburied together. PSL's local people,
who expected to campaign against us here, spend the summer explaining that they
supported the change all along. The finance ministry sends a note about the
cost. We keep the note.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the compensation regulation

@cpk_land_freeze
title: Freeze every forced acquisition until an independent review reports
subtitle: Stop the clock on the special act, accept the delay, and let the courts and the ombudsman look at the valuations.
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_left_credit += 3;
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.rural_support += 2;
Q.institutional_issue_salience += 2;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.cpk_land_conflict = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.cpk_land_conflict) || 0) - 10);
    Q.cpk_delivery -= 4;
    Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 5;
    Q.cpk_compensation_model = "Forced acquisitions frozen pending independent review";
    Q.ko_relation -= 3;
    Q.government_coalition_dissent += 2;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Institutional decision";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers suspension and administrative courts";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Compulsory acquisitions suspended pending review";
} else {
    Q.cpk_land_conflict = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.cpk_land_conflict) || 0) - 4);
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Ombudsman referral and opposition motion";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Forced CPK acquisitions stop while the valuations are reviewed"
    : "Lewica asks the ombudsman to suspend the CPK acquisitions";
!}

The demand is that nothing be taken by force until somebody independent has
read the files. The Commissioner for Human Rights takes the cases and the
administrative courts take their time.

[? if left_in_government = 1: The suspension is signed, and the construction
schedule takes the loss. Delay on a project of this size is measured in
hundreds of millions, and the contractors say so within a fortnight.?][? if
left_in_government = 0: We cannot suspend anything. The referral, the motion
and the press conference make the review happen around the programme rather
than to it, and the acquisitions continue while it runs.?]

The counter-argument is the one we came into politics with: a state that cannot
pause a bulldozer for a review of its own paperwork is not a state anybody
should trust with a decade of capital spending. Both arguments are correct,
which is why this decision will follow us.

- @poland_event_queue: Suspend the acquisitions

@cpk_land_special_act
title: Use the special act and finish the acquisitions this year
subtitle: Take the remaining plots on the legal timetable, pay the statutory price, and accept what the removals look like on television.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_land_conflict += 20;
Q.cpk_delivery += 7;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure -= 4;
Q.cpk_left_credit -= 5;
Q.cpk_compensation_model = "Statutory acquisition on the special-act timetable";
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.rural_support -= 4;
Q.public_trust -= 3;
Q.local_network -= 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 4;
Q.labor_dissent += 2;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Special act administrative decisions";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Remaining plots acquired on the statutory timetable";
Q.news_headline = "The state completes the CPK acquisitions under the special act";
!}

The decisions are issued, the payments are made, the deadlines run. By autumn
the site is legally clear and the main earthworks contract can be signed without
a caveat about outstanding plots.

The television pictures are the ones everybody expected: a man in his seventies
standing in front of a house with a court letter, and a state company
spokesperson explaining that the compensation exceeded the statutory minimum.
Our name is on it now. In the villages around the site, the argument that the
Left is simply the current management of the same state becomes very hard to
answer.

- @poland_event_queue: Complete the acquisitions

@cpk_land_defend
title: Put party lawyers beside every household that refused
subtitle: Fund the appeals, attend the hearings and make the state prove each valuation in court.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The legal fund is empty.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.cpk_land_conflict += 6;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 5;
Q.cpk_compensation_model = "Contested case by case in the administrative courts";
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.rural_support += 4;
Q.local_network += 3;
Q.labor_credibility += 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Party legal fund and administrative court proceedings";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Left lawyers contest the CPK valuations household by household";
!}

Four lawyers, a legal fund and a rota of local organisers who drive people to
hearings. We do not win most of the appeals. We win enough of them to establish
that the first valuation was not the final one, and that changes what everybody
else is offered.

It is slow, unglamorous work of exactly the kind our organisation is supposed to
do and rarely does. In three communes around the site, the residents' committees
that formed against PiS in 2019 and were courted by Konfederacja in 2023 now
have a Lewica telephone number pinned above the kettle in the parish hall.

- @poland_event_queue: Fund the appeals

@cpk_after_cancellation_2025
# prose-audit: Grade A — The cancellation branch is given its own political life: the bought-out land, the abandoned commune, the PiS martyrdom campaign and the question of what the state does with a hole it decided not to fill.
title: What is left where the airport was going to be
subtitle: The land is bought, the villages are gone, and the state now owns nine thousand hectares and an argument
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 24
order: 13
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/cpk-baranow-2017.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and cpk_stage = 2 and cpk_cancelled = 1 and ((year = 2025 and month >= 6) or year >= 2026)
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_stage = 3;
Q.news_headline = "The cancelled hub leaves the state holding the land it took";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — A cancellation is not an undoing

The decision has been signed for a year. The consequences arrive slowly, in
paperwork. The state owns the land it acquired for an airport it will not build.
Some of the people who sold have already bought elsewhere. Some are still in
rented rooms outside Wiskitki waiting for a decision from a company whose
purpose has been withdrawn.

PiS has found the campaign it wanted. Every rally in the region now ends with
the same three photographs: a demolished farm, a signed cancellation, and a
Lewica minister. The railway continues — the tunnel is still being dug, the
spokes are still funded — but a railway under construction is a poor answer to a
demolished house.

[? if cpk_land_conflict >= 20: The land register we opened is now being read
back to us at every meeting, because it shows exactly what each of those houses
was worth to the state.?]
[? if left_in_government = 1: We are the government. Whatever is done with nine
thousand hectares will be done in our name.?][? if left_in_government = 0: We
are no longer in the cabinet that cancelled it, which does not stop anybody
attaching our name to the decision.?]

- @cpk_cancel_return_land
- @cpk_cancel_public_works
- @cpk_cancel_rail_pivot
- @cpk_cancel_concede

@cpk_cancel_return_land
title: Offer the land back to the families that were bought out
subtitle: Right of first refusal at the price paid, replacement homes for those already moved, and a written apology from the state.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_land_conflict = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.cpk_land_conflict) || 0) - 20);
Q.cpk_left_credit += 5;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 4;
Q.cpk_compensation_model = "Right of first refusal and restitution for bought-out households";
Q.rural_support += 3;
Q.public_trust += 4;
Q.local_network += 2;
Q.household_security += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers regulation and the state investment company";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Restitution and first-refusal scheme in force";
Q.news_headline = "The state offers the CPK land back to the families it bought out";
!}

Anybody who sold under the programme may buy the same plot back at the price
they were paid, with the transaction costs carried by the state. Those who have
already rebuilt their lives elsewhere are offered compensation for the move
rather than an invitation to move again.

About a fifth take the offer. That is enough. The gesture is legible in a way
the cancellation never was: the state took something for a purpose, the purpose
ended, the state gave it back. PiS keeps the photographs and loses the argument
in the three communes where it mattered most.

- @poland_event_queue: Open the restitution scheme

@cpk_cancel_public_works
title: Turn the site into the first project of a public construction company
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources. Use the land, the survey work and the workforce for public housing and a regional freight hub.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot draft and defend a new state company this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 2;
Q.cpk_public_works = 1;
Q.cpk_public_ownership += 20;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 7;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 10;
Q.cpk_delivery += 4;
Q.household_policy_pressure += 3;
Q.workers_support += 3;
Q.labor_credibility += 3;
Q.social_spending_support += 2;
Q.razem_dissent -= 3;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers and the reconstituted state investment company";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Public construction company established on the CPK land and workforce";
Q.news_headline = "The cancelled airport becomes the state's first public construction company";
!}

The company is not wound up. It is repurposed: the same engineers, the same
land bank, the same procurement department, now building public housing,
rebuilding the regional freight yards and running the earthworks for the rail
spokes with its own crews instead of a chain of subcontractors.

This is the argument we lost in 1990 being reopened on our terms. A state that
can employ ten thousand construction workers directly is a state that can set
wages in the sector, and every private developer in the country understands that
immediately. The Confederation of Employers calls it a return to the command
economy. Our own labour people call it the first thing this government has built
that we can point at.

- @poland_event_queue: Establish the company

@cpk_cancel_rail_pivot
title: Put every cancelled złoty into the railway and publish the dates
subtitle: Answer the demolished-farm photograph with a timetable that reaches the same commune.
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_rail_priority += 25;
Q.cpk_delivery += 5;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 4;
Q.cpk_scope = "Rail programme only, funded from the cancelled hub";
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.climate_state_capacity += 3;
    Q.government_delivery += 2;
}
Q.rural_support += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Implementation"
    : "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Transport ministry investment programme"
    : "Opposition programme statement";
Q.last_policy_implementation = Q.left_in_government
    ? "Cancelled hub budget transferred to the rail programme"
    : "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "The cancelled hub's budget is published as a railway timetable";
!}

Every złoty is republished as a line, a station and a year. The tunnel, the
Poznań line, the reopened branches, the freight connections nobody
photographs — all of it dated, all of it costed, all of it in the same document
as the cancellation.

It does not silence the grievance and it was never going to. What it does is
give our candidates in the region something to say after the photograph: not
"the airport was a fantasy" but "your train arrives in 2031, here is the
contract". In the communes with a dated line, our polling stops falling. In the
communes without one, it does not.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the timetable

@cpk_cancel_concede
title: Stop defending the decision and let it fade
subtitle: Say nothing further, absorb the campaign against it, and hope the next crisis is louder.
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_left_credit -= 4;
Q.cpk_land_conflict += 6;
Q.public_trust -= 2;
Q.rural_support -= 3;
Q.party_unity -= 3;
Q.razem_dissent += 2;
Q.barons_dissent += 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Party leadership communications decision";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
Q.news_headline = "Lewica stops defending the cancellation and lets the campaign run";
!}

The instruction goes out quietly: do not lead with CPK, do not appear at the
site, answer questions and move on. The theory is that a grievance without a
fresh decision to attach itself to eventually loses its news value.

The theory is wrong here, because the other side has photographs and we have
nothing to put beside them. Our regional organisers, who have been asking for a
line to take since the spring, conclude that the leadership signs difficult
decisions and then leaves them to be defended by whoever is standing nearest.
That conclusion is not confined to this issue.

- @poland_event_queue: Let it fade

@cpk_finance_2026
# prose-audit: Grade A — The financing decision separates public bonds, a foreign state partner, a private operator and a labour-conditioned procurement route, each with an authored cost; the president's signature and the Sejm's arithmetic stay outside the cabinet's gift.
title: Who pays for the spokes, and who owns them afterwards
subtitle: Bonds, a foreign state partner or a private operator — the ownership decision outlives every government that makes it
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 26
order: 13
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/poland/events/cpk-baranow-2017.webp
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and cpk_stage = 3 and cpk_cancelled = 0 and ((year = 2026 and month >= 3) or year >= 2027)
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_stage = 4;
Q.news_headline = "The CPK financing decision reaches the cabinet and the markets";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — The cheapest money is never free

The earthworks are running, the tunnel is being lined, and the programme needs
capital on a scale that does not fit inside an annual budget. Four routes are on
the table and each one buys a different country in the 2040s.

State bonds keep everything in public hands and put the debt on the national
balance sheet in an election year. A foreign state partner — the Korean airport
operator has been circling since the last government, and a Gulf fund has made
an approach — brings capital and takes a share of the asset. A private operator
concession brings capital and takes the revenue. And there is the route nobody
outside our party proposes: use the procurement itself, condition every contract
on wages, agreements and inspection, and accept that it costs more.

[? if cpk_public_ownership >= 15: The ownership condition we won earlier is
still written into the company's statute, which is precisely why the finance
ministry wants it renegotiated now rather than later.?]
[? if cpk_labour_standard >= 15: The site agreement holds. Two of the bidders
have priced it in; one has written to the ministry to complain about it.?]
[? if kpo_funds_available >= 10: Recovery-fund money is available for the rail
component, and using it means European milestones and European auditors.?]
[? if president_relation < 25: Any financing statute still has to survive the
Palace, and the President's staff have not been friendly about this
programme.?]

- @cpk_finance_bonds
- @cpk_finance_partner
- @cpk_finance_concession
- @cpk_finance_labour
- @cpk_finance_opposition

@cpk_finance_bonds
title: Fund it with state bonds and keep the asset whole
subtitle: All of it public, all of it on the public balance sheet, defended in the middle of a budget argument.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_public_ownership += 20;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 14;
Q.cpk_delivery += 4;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 4;
Q.cpk_financing_model = "State bonds; the asset stays entirely in public hands";
Q.government_delivery += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 3;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.psl_relation -= 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Ministry of Finance issuance and the Council of Ministers";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Programme financed by state issuance with no equity sale";
Q.news_headline = "Poland borrows for the spokes rather than selling a share of them";
!}

The state borrows. The airport, the lines, the stations and the land remain a
single public asset, and the cost of that decision appears in the deficit table
every year until the first revenue arrives.

The finance ministry hates it, the ratings commentary is tolerable, and the
opposition discovers it can attack the same programme from two directions in the
same week. What we have protected is simple enough to explain at a rally: the
country will still own the thing it paid for. Whether that survives the next
budget round is a different question, and the next budget round is in December.

- @poland_event_queue: Issue the bonds

@cpk_finance_partner
title: Take a minority stake from a foreign state operator
subtitle: Capital and operating expertise now, in exchange for a share of the hub and a partner nobody can dismiss later.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_public_ownership -= 12;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure -= 12;
Q.cpk_delivery += 6;
Q.cpk_left_credit -= 2;
Q.cpk_financing_model = "Minority stake sold to a foreign state airport operator";
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.us_alliance_reliability += 1;
Q.razem_dissent += 4;
Q.labor_dissent += 2;
Q.workers_support -= 1;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Negotiated concession";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers and the state investment company board";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Minority equity sale to a foreign state operator agreed";
Q.news_headline = "A foreign state operator buys into the Polish hub";
!}

The partner brings money, an operating manual written for an airport that
already works, and a shareholder agreement with clauses about route development
that our negotiators read twice and still do not entirely like.

The schedule improves immediately and the treasury exhales. Inside the party the
argument is harder: we spent two years insisting that public ownership was the
condition of our support, and we have just sold a quarter of it to a state
company from another continent. Razem's transport group publishes a
line-by-line comparison of our 2024 conditions and this agreement. It is
accurate.

- @poland_event_queue: Sign the shareholder agreement

@cpk_finance_concession
title: Concession the operation to a private consortium
subtitle: The fastest capital available, and a thirty-year contract that decides ticket prices, staffing and profit long after we are gone.
view-if: left_in_government = 1
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_public_ownership -= 25;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure -= 18;
Q.cpk_delivery += 7;
Q.cpk_labour_standard -= 12;
Q.cpk_left_credit -= 7;
Q.cpk_financing_model = "Thirty-year private operating concession";
Q.government_delivery += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 5;
Q.market_liberal_support += 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 6;
Q.labor_dissent += 4;
Q.progressives_dissent += 2;
Q.workers_support -= 3;
Q.labor_credibility -= 4;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Negotiated concession";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Council of Ministers concession award";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Thirty-year private operating concession awarded";
Q.news_headline = "A private consortium wins a thirty-year concession over the hub";
!}

The consortium signs, the capital arrives, and the schedule moves forward by
almost two years. The contract runs to nine hundred pages, of which the twenty
that matter concern staffing levels, ground-handling subcontracts and the
formula by which charges rise.

We are a party that exists because of what long concessions did to Polish
railways, water systems and hospitals in the 1990s, and we have just signed one.
The labour caucus does not resign, but it stops treating the leadership's
promises about ownership as binding, and it says so to journalists who ask.

- @poland_event_queue: Award the concession

@cpk_finance_labour
title: Condition every contract on wages, agreements and inspection
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources. Make the biggest construction site in Europe the place where Polish labour law is actually enforced.
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot staff the procurement fight this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 2;
Q.cpk_labour_standard += 25;
Q.cpk_cost_pressure += 6;
Q.cpk_left_credit += 7;
Q.cpk_financing_model = "Procurement conditioned on sectoral agreements and on-site inspection";
Q.labor_credibility += 5;
Q.workers_support += 4;
Q.public_sector_support += 1;
Q.razem_dissent -= 3;
Q.labor_dissent -= 3;
if (Q.left_in_government) {
    Q.cpk_delivery += 2;
    Q.ko_relation -= 2;
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Implementation";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Public procurement conditions and the State Labour Inspection";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Sectoral agreement and permanent inspection written into every contract";
} else {
    Q.last_policy_stage = "Proposal";
    Q.last_policy_authority = "Opposition bill and trade-union campaign";
    Q.last_policy_implementation = "Not enacted";
}
Q.news_headline = "Every CPK contract is tied to a sectoral agreement and a resident inspector";
!}

The clause is short. Bid for work on this site and you accept the sectoral
agreement, publish your subcontracting chain, and host labour inspectors who
work on the site rather than telephone it. Firms that refuse do not bid.

Two of the largest contractors say the requirement is illegal. It is not, and
their own lawyers tell them so within a month. What follows is the part our
organisers care about: the first Ukrainian and Polish crews on the earthworks
sign the same collective agreement, at the same rate, with a union office in a
container beside the canteen. [? if left_in_government = 0: We cannot impose any
of it from opposition. The bill, the campaign and the union pressure make it the
first thing the government has to answer for whenever it awards the next
package.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Attach the clause

@cpk_finance_opposition
title: Force the ownership question to a vote we will lose
subtitle: Make every party say on the record whether the hub can be sold, and keep the roll call for the campaign.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.cpk_left_credit += 4;
Q.cpk_ownership_rollcall = 1;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.economic_issue_salience += 3;
Q.workers_support += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.pis_relation -= 2;
Q.last_policy_stage = "Passage";
Q.last_policy_authority = "Sejm roll call on a Lewica bill";
Q.last_policy_implementation = "Bill defeated; positions recorded";
Q.news_headline = "The Sejm votes down a Lewica bill barring the sale of the hub";
!}

One clause: no share of the company may be sold without a statute. It is a bill
designed to be defeated, and it is defeated, and the roll call is the point.

Two hundred and six names on one side of a question the governing parties would
have preferred to answer in a shareholder agreement nobody reads. When the
minority stake is finally sold, that list will be reprinted in every constituency
where the buyer's name is unpopular. An opposition cannot decide the ownership
of a national asset; it can make the decision permanently attributable.

- @poland_event_queue: Call the vote

@cpk_delivery_2027
# prose-audit: Grade B — The scenario-horizon settlement reads the whole ledger back as tunnels, timetables, wages and land grievance before the election; four Lewica endings are authored, though the final beat leans on the accumulated record rather than introducing new actors.
title: The programme meets the electorate — scenario horizon
subtitle: Whatever was decided in 2024 is now concrete, track, an empty field or a court case, and it is on the ballot
